<p>In 1897, to help inaugurate New York City&#8217;s new pneumatic mail system, officials sent a live black cat in a canister from the Produce Exchange Post Office on Bowling Green to the General Post Office on Park Row, a distance of 3,750 feet.</p>
<p>&#8220;How it could live after being shot at terrific speed from Station P in the Produce Exchange Building, making several turns before reaching Broadway and Park Row, I cannot conceive, but it did,&#8221; recalled postal supervisor Howard Wallace Connelly. &#8220;It seemed to be dazed for a minute or two but started to run and was quickly secured and placed in a basket that had been provided for that purpose.&#8221;</p>
<p>At its peak the system would carry 95,000 letters a day through 27 miles of tubes connecting 23 post offices. It was discontinued in 1953, outmoded by the growing volume of mail and the advent of the automobile.</p>
